      "content": {
        "markdown": "The simplest form of invalidation is not to invalidate at all: every cached object carries a time to live (TTL), and the edge discards or rechecks its copy once the TTL has passed. The origin sets the TTL with the `Cache-Control` header; for example, `Cache-Control: max-age=300` lets an edge location keep a copy for 300 seconds. TTL expiry needs no coordination between the origin and the edge, which is why every CDN uses it as the default. When a TTL runs out, the edge does not always download the whole object again: if the origin sent an `ETag`, the edge can ask whether the copy changed and receive a short `304 Not Modified` answer when it has not."
      },
      "stableKey": "p1"
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Its weakness is that the TTL is a guess made in advance. If an editor corrects a headline 60 seconds after publishing, readers can keep seeing the old headline for up to the remaining 240 seconds of a 300-second TTL, and nothing the origin does will shorten that wait. Shortening the TTL narrows the window but sends more requests back to the origin: when Larkspur dropped its article TTL from 300 to 30 seconds, origin traffic rose roughly eightfold."
      },
      "stableKey": "p2"
    }
  ],
  "relationships": [],
  "metadata": {
    "takeaway": "TTL-based caching is simple but can lead to stale content or increased origin traffic."
  }
}
